      "objectiveEn": "Identify the grammatical gender (masculine, feminine, or neuter) of a Russian noun from its ending, the foundation for all noun, adjective, and verb agreement at A1.",
      "teach": [
       {
        "kind": "concept",
        "headingEn": "Every Russian noun has a gender",
        "en": "Russian nouns are masculine, feminine, or neuter. For most nouns you can tell the gender just by looking at the last letter of the dictionary (nominative singular) form. Gender matters because adjectives, possessives, and past-tense verbs must agree with the noun."
       },
       {
        "kind": "concept",
        "headingEn": "The basic ending rules",
        "en": "Masculine: ends in a consonant (no vowel), or in -й. Feminine: ends in -а or -я. Neuter: ends in -о or -е. A final soft sign -ь can be masculine OR feminine and must be memorized per word."
       },
       {
        "kind": "example",
        "ru": "стол",
        "translit": "stol",
        "en": "table (masculine — ends in a consonant)"
       },
       {
        "kind": "example",
        "ru": "книга",
        "translit": "kniga",
        "en": "book (feminine — ends in -а)"
       },
       {
        "kind": "example",
        "ru": "окно",
        "translit": "okno",
        "en": "window (neuter — ends in -о)"
       },
       {
        "kind": "example",
        "ru": "море",
        "translit": "more",
        "en": "sea (neuter — ends in -е)"
       },
       {
        "kind": "tip",
        "en": "The soft sign -ь is unpredictable. Learn it with the word: ночь (night) is feminine, but день (day) is masculine. When in doubt, check a dictionary."
       },
       {
        "kind": "tip",
        "en": "Natural gender wins over the ending for people: папа (dad) and дядя (uncle) end in -а but are masculine because they name males."
       }
      ],
