      "objectiveEn": "Form the nominative plural of masculine and feminine nouns using -ы or -и, and apply the spelling rule that requires -и after certain consonants.",
      "teach": [
       {
        "kind": "concept",
        "headingEn": "Default plural endings for masculine and feminine nouns",
        "en": "To make most masculine and feminine nouns plural, drop a final vowel if there is one and add -ы. Masculine consonant nouns simply add -ы. Feminine -а nouns replace -а with -ы. Soft nouns ending in -й, -я, or -ь take -и instead of -ы."
       },
       {
        "kind": "example",
        "ru": "стол → столы",
        "translit": "stol → stoly",
        "en": "table → tables (consonant + -ы)"
       },
       {
        "kind": "example",
        "ru": "книга → книги",
        "translit": "kniga → knigi",
        "en": "book → books (-а replaced; -и required after г, see spelling rule)"
       },
       {
        "kind": "example",
        "ru": "неделя → недели",
        "translit": "nedelya → nedeli",
        "en": "week → weeks (soft -я replaced by -и)"
       },
       {
        "kind": "letter",
        "ru": "к г х ж ш щ ч",
        "translit": "k g kh zh sh shch ch",
        "en": "the seven 'spelling-rule' consonants",
        "noteEn": "After these seven letters you must write -и, never -ы. This is the famous Russian spelling rule. So книга → книги (not книгы) and нож → ножи (not ножы)."
       },
       {
        "kind": "tip",
        "en": "Quick test: if the stem ends in к, г, х, ж, ш, щ, or ч, the plural is -и. Otherwise a hard masculine/feminine noun takes -ы."
       },
       {
        "kind": "example",
        "ru": "нож → ножи",
        "translit": "nozh → nozhi",
        "en": "knife → knives (-и after ж by the spelling rule)"
       }
      ],
