      "titleEn": "Subject Pronouns: я, ты, он, она…",
      "objectiveEn": "Recognize and use the six Russian personal subject pronouns (я, ты, он, она, оно, мы, вы, они), understand the ты/вы distinction (informal vs. formal/plural), and know which pronoun replaces a noun by its gender.",
      "teach": [
       {
        "kind": "concept",
        "headingEn": "The personal pronouns",
        "en": "Russian subject pronouns are: я (I), ты (you, informal singular), он (he/it-masc), она (she/it-fem), оно (it-neuter), мы (we), вы (you, formal or plural), они (they). Unlike English, Russian has TWO words for 'you': ты for one friend/child/family member, and вы for a stranger, an older person, or more than one person."
       },
       {
        "kind": "letter",
        "ru": "я",
        "translit": "ya",
        "en": "I",
        "noteEn": "Always lowercase mid-sentence — Russian does NOT capitalize 'я' the way English capitalizes 'I'."
       },
       {
        "kind": "example",
        "ru": "Я студент. Ты студентка.",
        "translit": "Ya student. Ty studentka.",
        "en": "I am a student (m). You are a student (f). — Note there is no word for 'am/are'; Russian drops the present-tense 'to be'."
       },
       {
        "kind": "example",
        "ru": "Он врач, а она учительница.",
        "translit": "On vrach, a ona uchitelnitsa.",
        "en": "He is a doctor, and she is a teacher."
       },
       {
        "kind": "concept",
        "headingEn": "он / она / оно replace nouns by gender",
        "en": "Any noun can be replaced by он, она, or оно based on its grammatical gender — not just people. A masculine noun (стол 'table') becomes он, a feminine noun (книга 'book') becomes она, and a neuter noun (окно 'window') becomes оно. So 'the table' is он, exactly like 'he'."
       },
       {
        "kind": "tip",
        "en": "Use вы (capitalized as Вы in polite letters) with anyone you'd address by their first name and patronymic, with shopkeepers, officials, and elders. Using ты with a stranger can sound rude."
       }
      ],
