      "titleEn": "Dative Endings: Drag & Drop",
      "objectiveEn": "Practice the dative singular case by dragging the correct endings onto noun stems. Masculine and neuter nouns take -у (or -ю when soft); feminine nouns take -е (or -и for soft/abstract types). The dative answers \"to whom?\" and is used after verbs of giving and the prepositions к and по.",
      "teach": [
       {
        "kind": "concept",
        "headingEn": "What the dative is for",
        "en": "The dative case marks the recipient or indirect object — the person or thing something is given, sent, or said TO. It also follows the prepositions к (toward) and по (along / according to).",
        "ru": "Я дал книгу брату.",
        "translit": "Ya dal knigu bratu.",
        "noteEn": "\"I gave the book to (my) brother.\" — брат → брату."
       },
       {
        "kind": "letter",
        "headingEn": "The endings",
        "en": "Masculine & neuter: add -у (hard) or -ю (soft, after -й/-ь or stems ending in soft consonants). Feminine: change -а/-я to -е; but -ь and -ия feminines take -и.",
        "ru": "стол → столу, море → морю, сестра → сестре, площадь → площади",
        "noteEn": "Watch for the soft sign and -ия nouns — they break the simple -е pattern."
       },
       {
        "kind": "tip",
        "headingEn": "Don't confuse it with other cases",
        "en": "The feminine dative -е looks just like the prepositional -е, and the masculine -а (genitive) is a common trap. Ask \"to whom / to what?\" — if yes, it's dative.",
        "ru": "подойти к доске",
        "translit": "podoyti k doske",
        "noteEn": "\"to go up to the board\" — доска → доске after к."
       }
      ],
