      "titleEn": "Relative Adverbs: где, куда, откуда, когда",
      "objectiveEn": "Use где/куда/откуда/когда as relative connectors of place and time, and contrast them with в/на + котором, recognizing when each is required or interchangeable.",
      "teach": [
       {
        "kind": "concept",
        "headingEn": "Place relatives: где, куда, откуда",
        "en": "When the relative clause describes a PLACE, you can use the relative adverbs где ('where', location — no motion), куда ('to where', motion toward), and откуда ('from where', motion from), instead of в/на + который. The choice mirrors the motion logic of Russian: где = at/in (static), куда = to (destination), откуда = from (origin). These are invariable — they never decline."
       },
       {
        "kind": "example",
        "ru": "Это город, где я вырос.",
        "translit": "Eto gorod, gde ya vyros.",
        "en": "This is the city where I grew up. — Static location → где. Equivalent to 'город, в котором я вырос'. With где, no preposition or case agreement is needed because где already means 'in/at the place'."
       },
       {
        "kind": "example",
        "ru": "Деревня, куда мы едем, очень маленькая.",
        "translit": "Derevnya, kuda my edem, ochen' malen'kaya.",
        "en": "The village (to) where we are going is very small. — Motion toward a destination → куда (= 'в которую'). Using где here would be wrong because there is movement toward the village (едем)."
       },
       {
        "kind": "concept",
        "headingEn": "когда and the place vs. который choice",
        "en": "Когда ('when') connects a clause describing a TIME: 'день, когда мы встретились' ('the day when we met'). For place, где/куда/откуда and в/на + который are often interchangeable when the antecedent is a literal physical location (город, дом, страна). But когда and the place-adverbs are the natural, idiomatic choice. Use который (not где) when the antecedent is NOT a place — e.g. an organization or abstract noun acting as a grammatical object."
       },
       {
        "kind": "example",
        "ru": "Я помню день, когда мы познакомились.",
        "translit": "Ya pomnyu den', kogda my poznakomilis'.",
        "en": "I remember the day when we met. — Time antecedent день → когда. You could also say 'день, в который мы познакомились', but когда is more idiomatic for time expressions."
       },
       {
        "kind": "concept",
        "headingEn": "When you must use который, not где",
        "en": "Где replaces only a LOCATIVE 'in/at which'. If который plays another role (subject, object, possessor, or takes a different preposition), где cannot substitute. 'Компания, в которой я работаю' can become 'компания, где я работаю', but 'компания, которая обанкротилась' ('the company that went bankrupt') cannot use где, because который is the subject there, not a location."
       },
       {
        "kind": "tip",
        "en": "Decision guide: TIME antecedent → когда. PLACE antecedent + 'static in/at' → где (or в/на котором). PLACE + 'to' → куда (or в/на + accusative который). PLACE + 'from' → откуда (or из/с + genitive который). Any other grammatical role → который. Quick motion test: stay = где, go-to = куда, come-from = откуда."
       }
      ],
