      "titleEn": "Theme and Rheme: Word Order as Information Flow",
      "objectiveEn": "By the end you can explain how Russian word order encodes what is given (theme) versus what is new/emphasized (rheme), and choose the order that fits the communicative focus.",
      "teach": [
       {
        "kind": "concept",
        "headingEn": "Why Russian word order is 'free' but never random",
        "en": "Russian case endings mark who-does-what-to-whom, so word order is not needed for grammar the way it is in English. Instead, word order carries INFORMATION STRUCTURE. The neutral pattern is theme (тема) first — the known, already-established topic — and rheme (рема) last — the new, communicatively most important information. In speech the rheme also carries the main stress (intonation centre IK-1). So the SAME words in a different order answer a DIFFERENT question."
       },
       {
        "kind": "example",
        "ru": "Письмо принёс почтальон.",
        "translit": "Pis'mó prinyós pochtal'ón.",
        "en": "It was the postman who brought the letter. (Theme: the letter, already known; rheme/new info at the end: the postman — answers 'Who brought the letter?')"
       },
       {
        "kind": "example",
        "ru": "Почтальон принёс письмо.",
        "translit": "Pochtal'ón prinyós pis'mó.",
        "en": "The postman brought a letter. (Theme: the postman; rheme at the end: a letter — answers 'What did the postman bring?')"
       },
       {
        "kind": "concept",
        "headingEn": "The default and how to break it deliberately",
        "en": "Neutral declarative order is Subject–Verb–Object (S–V–O), with the rheme falling naturally at the end. To highlight a different element, move it to the END (the rheme slot) — not to the front, as English does with 'It is X that...'. Fronting an element in Russian instead makes it the theme or gives it contrastive emphasis with a special falling-rising intonation."
       },
       {
        "kind": "tip",
        "en": "Test for the rheme by asking 'What question does this sentence answer?' The word that answers the question-word (кто? что? когда? куда?) is the rheme and normally sits at the end. If a translation exercise gives you a wh-question as context, put the answering element last."
       }
      ],
