        "en": "Russian joins clauses three ways. (1) СЛОЖНОСОЧИНЁННОЕ (compound): two equal clauses linked by coordinating conjunctions и, а, но, или, да, однако. (2) СЛОЖНОПОДЧИНЁННОЕ (complex): a main clause plus a dependent clause introduced by a subordinating conjunction or relative word (что, чтобы, потому что, если, когда, который, хотя). (3) БЕССОЮЗНОЕ (asyndetic): clauses joined only by punctuation, no conjunction. Crucially, a comma is placed before almost every conjunction joining two clauses — including before 'и' when it links two full clauses with different subjects."
