      "titleEn": "Subtext: Reading What the Text Does Not Say",
      "objectiveEn": "By the end you can detect implicit meaning (подтекст) in literary prose, distinguishing what is stated from what is communicated through silence, juxtaposition, and detail.",
      "teach": [
       {
        "kind": "concept",
        "headingEn": "Subtext (подтекст) is the load-bearing layer",
        "en": "In sophisticated Russian prose — Chekhov is the canonical case — the most important information is rarely stated outright. Подтекст is meaning that the reader must reconstruct from what is withheld, from a seemingly trivial detail, or from the gap between two sentences. A character may talk about the weather while the scene is 'really' about a marriage falling apart. To read at C1, you must stop treating the surface proposition as the message and ask: why THIS detail, why THIS silence, why HERE?"
       },
       {
        "kind": "concept",
        "headingEn": "Mechanisms that generate subtext",
        "en": "Watch for: (1) juxtaposition — two unrelated facts placed side by side so the reader infers a causal or emotional link the narrator never asserts; (2) the telling detail (деталь) — a single physical object that carries the weight of an unspoken state; (3) ellipsis and understatement — characters who say less than the situation demands, where the omission IS the point; (4) the disproportion between event and reaction. The text's reticence (недоговорённость) is not a defect — it is the instrument."
       },
       {
        "kind": "example",
        "ru": "«Дома он застал её спокойной и весёлой. Только чай она в тот вечер пить не стала».",
        "translit": "Doma on zastal eyo spokoynoy i vesyoloy. Tolʹko chay ona v tot vecher pitʹ ne stala.",
        "en": "'At home he found her calm and cheerful. Only — that evening she did not drink her tea.' The surface says all is well; the lone detail ('did not drink her tea') quietly contradicts 'calm and cheerful' and signals concealed distress. The word «только» (only) flags the crack in the facade."
       },
       {
        "kind": "example",
        "ru": "«— Хорошая погода сегодня. — Да, — сказал он и стал смотреть в окно».",
        "translit": "— Khoroshaya pogoda segodnya. — Da, — skazal on i stal smotretʹ v okno.",
        "en": "'\"Nice weather today.\" \"Yes,\" he said, and began looking out the window.' Nothing is stated, yet the banality of the exchange plus the turn to the window conveys avoidance — the conversation is a screen for what cannot be said."
       },
       {
        "kind": "tip",
        "en": "When a detail seems gratuitously specific or a reaction seems too small for the event, that disproportion is usually the door to the subtext. Ask what emotional truth the surface is politely covering."
       }
      ],
